WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.500
The human brain is the most powerful 
supercomputer in the world.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.860
All right, let’s see this electrical headquarters
of yours in operation.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.020
It helps us navigate our environment by carrying out

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:15.120
about one thousand trillion 
logical operations per second.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:21.780
It’s compact, uses less power than a lightbulb
and has potentially endless storage.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.060
The human brain is really one of the most
complex systems that we can imagine.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:34.060
We have a fundamental lack in our understanding
of the way the components in the brain interact.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.960
But it is this very interaction that generates
cognition and consciousness.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.120
All these mind-boggling intricacies have driven
our fascination with the brain

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.240
and for centuries we’ve been trying to map 
and understand it.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.180
And most recently - replicate it.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.500
The brain is certainly a computer

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.480
that has been evolving 
for nearly 4 billion years.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.909
And the more we learn about the brain, the
more we're able to incorporate the smart ways

00:01:01.909 --> 00:01:04.740
that it does computation 
into our artificial devices.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.760
Scientists are beginning to agree that to
realize our technological dreams,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.800
we need to build computers that work like our brains.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:20.300
One day these computers could in turn help
us unlock more secrets of cognition.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.520
The brain is packed with neuron cells that
constantly communicate with each other

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.680
through electrical pulses, known as spikes.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.880
Each neuron releases molecules that act as
messengers and control if the electrical pulse

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.600
is passed along the chain.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.620
This relay race is happening simultaneously
throughout billions of neurons.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.360
Much like the zeros and ones of the computer
world, this is the basic language of the brain.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.220
But understanding all of this isn’t enough.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.540
We’ve still only scratched the surface when
it comes to figuring out how the brain works.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:05.180
The more I'm working on the brain,
the more I understand

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.380
how complex it is, how difficult it is.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:16.740
Many relatively easy cognitive functions cannot
really be understood at the level of cells.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.160
The human brain 
is one of the biggest secrets

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.260
and mysteries that we have,

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.280
despite many years of intensive work.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.780
Katrin Amunts is at the helm of 
the Human Brain Project,

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.600
a 10 year long attempt at studying the brain.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.400
With researchers collaborating across 100 universities,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.240
the project is expected to cost around €1 billion.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:46.960
Professor Amunts and her team are working
on one part of it, a 3D digital brain atlas.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:52.600
They are creating three different high resolution
maps - one of neurons;

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.940
one of their connections - which uses different
colors to indicate the orientation of neurons’ branches;

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.600
and one map of the receptors 
for the messenger molecules.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:08.820
When we think about an atlas of the world,
we can map all the different countries.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:15.870
But then we can also see there are maps illustrating
the level above the sea or the temperature.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:19.020
And it's a little bit like what we have 
in the human brain.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.280
There are different aspects.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.870
We want to understand where the cells are located.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.200
We want to understand where certain areas
are located, how they are connected,

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.700
what is their molecular profile,

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.580
what is their gene expression that 
is important for function.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:38.880
So there is not one single aspect that can
explain everything in the human brain.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:45.500
So that means we need different types of maps
that reflect different aspects of brain organization.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.080
To create the maps, the team is scanning slices
of post-mortem brains.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.800
We get brains from body donors and process them,

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:02.170
embed them in paraffin, and then cut them
into 20-micrometer-thick sections.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:07.320
20 micrometers, this is approximately like
thickness of one hair so this is very thin.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.760
One brain has approximately 7000 sections.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:16.020
These sections can then be analyzed under
the microscope and we can then

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.900
reconstruct the areas in 3D.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.360
Much like a fingerprint, every brain is unique,
so to account for these differences,

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.740
the team scan 10 brains for each of their maps.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:33.160
This generates petabytes of data that’s
analyzed with the help of AI and used to run

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.660
brain simulations on supercomputers

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:37.759
but even the supercomputers struggle.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:42.320
So to further our understanding of the brain,
we need better machines.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.439
We cannot make our chips much faster 
without them melting,

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.420
unless we designed completely new architectures.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:53.599
We cannot make our components much smaller
because then we reach component sizes

00:04:53.599 --> 00:04:55.669
where quantum effects take over.

00:04:55.669 --> 00:04:58.420
So the computation becomes too imprecise 
to be practical.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.960
We need to find better solutions in order
to increase our computational power.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:09.520
Mihai Petrovici, like many other scientists
in the field, thinks that

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.960
modeling computer hardware 
on our brains is the way to go.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:17.140
It will not only increase the speed and efficiency
of future machines,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.640
but also help build better AI.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.200
There are certainly things that computers
do much better than brains,

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.380
such as adding or multiplying big numbers,
because this is what they were designed for.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.820
Intricate problems in mathematics are accurately
solved in the minute fraction

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.700
of the time required for a human calculation.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:41.420
There is no evolutionary pressure for us to
be able to multiply big numbers.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.460
Otherwise, certainly our brains would be able to do it.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.080
However, there is a strong evolutionary pressure
to recognize your surroundings,

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.760
to be able to build an internal model 
of your surroundings.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.420
When you hear a noise in the bushes, to be
able to imagine that maybe there's a predator there.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:59.840
To be able to recognize faces in order to
live in a society

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.200
where people can actually communicate and cooperate.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:08.080
And this is what evolution has made our brains excel at.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:13.260
This ability to build an internal model of
the world, to have, sort of,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.300
the world inside your heads,

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.319
to imagine what is happening around you 
even if you don't see it,

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:24.600
this is of critical importance for a true
artificial intelligence.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:31.620
AI like Google image recognition, Alexa or
the autopilot in a self-driving car

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:34.400
all work thanks to neural networks,

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.860
software which already tries to imitate 
the way our brain recognizes patterns.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:44.680
One thing that today's artificial intelligence
needs in order to be able to perform

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.100
whatever task it was designed for,

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:48.780
is a lot of examples.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:52.420
So in order for Google, for example, to be
able to show you pictures of cats,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.200
whenever you type in cat,

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:58.240
it needs to have seen millions of images of cats.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:03.180
That is certainly not how we humans operate and learn.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.600
When you show a child, for example, a cat
or whatever other new thing,

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.219
it just needs to see it a couple of times
in order to quickly grasp the main features

00:07:13.219 --> 00:07:15.119
that are specific for that animal

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.900
and then recognize it whenever it sees another
individual of the species.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:26.880
The scientists at Heidelberg University are
working on a different part of the Human Brain Project.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.040
They’re using the brain maps developed by
Professor Amunts’ team

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:36.100
to build computer hardware they hope will help AI 
learn like our brains do.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:42.980
This new hardware is called neuromorphic which
means formed like neurons or like the brain.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.460
Actually, none of what you see here on the
outside is really neuromorphic.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:52.259
You might be tempted to think that this is
more or less like the machine that you have

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.460
at home on or under your desk.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:58.699
This would be true for the outside components

00:07:58.699 --> 00:08:03.749
but at the heart of the system, there lies
a piece of hardware that is fundamentally

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:06.999
radically different from the chips in your computer,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.180
and that is the neuromorphic heart of the system.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.920
The microchips on these wafers look nothing
like the entangled web of neurons

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.440
that we have in our heads.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.880
But each component communicates like an individual
neuron

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:24.120
by sending along spikes of electricity 
to their many partners.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.640
This design immensely increases the operating speed.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:36.340
neuromorphic hardware generates results 
10 million times faster than conventional hardware.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:41.520
We certainly believe that this will become
a big thing, we will see many applications

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:45.140
of these systems for everyday tasks.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:50.160
One of them would be face recognition, pattern
recognition in general,

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:54.420
speech recognition, the ability to read texts.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:59.040
The ultimate goal, of course, is to create
true artificial intelligence.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:07.400
But it's really hard to say by when we will be able to actually copy the brain in an artificial substrate.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:13.340
What we can certainly do and what we are doing
right now is - understand

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:16.240
particular aspects of computation in the brain.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:21.260
The 4 million artificial neurons packed into
this neuromorphic computer

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:26.180
are just a tiny fraction of 
the 86 billion neurons in the human brain.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:30.220
Still, it’s a big step forward for the machines.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:37.540
Even though our knowledge of the brain has
increased over the last few decades,

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:39.060
it’s still fragmented.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:43.940
If the Human Brain Project is successful,
it could bring this knowledge together

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:48.220
and encourage research and collaboration across
different scientific fields.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:52.580
And so this effort could be just the beginning
of the journey.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:58.960
Better understanding the human brain, is really
one of the challenges of the 21st century.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:05.660
We have an increasing amount of people suffering
from neurodegenerative diseases,

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:09.580
suffering from major depression, 
other psychiatric diseases.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:16.500
We need to have new tools to diagnose and
have better therapies for these brain diseases.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:19.640
And since we are living in an aging population,

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:23.960
these diseases, of course, 
play a major role in the future.

